  "summary": "Painting traditions of Greece, from Neolithic and Bronze Age frescoes through ancient vase painting to modern Greek art.",
  "description": "Greek painting spans millennia of artistic production in the Greek world. It begins with Neolithic and Bronze Age frescoes — notably the well-preserved wall paintings of Akrotiri on Thera — and flows into the celebrated ancient Greek vase painting, where black-figure, red-figure, and white-ground techniques turned ceramic surfaces into narrative canvases. Hellenistic painting expanded the repertoire with perspective, landscape, and genre scenes, while Byzantine and post-Byzantine traditions carried Greek painting into the icon and fresco traditions of the Orthodox world. Modern Greek art, from the Munich School to contemporary practice, continues the lineage. This index record points to the major sub-traditions: Greek art, Greek Bronze Age art, Greek Neolithic art, Ancient Greek art, Ancient Greek vase painting, East Greek vase painting, Hellenistic art, and Modern Greek art.",
